eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}+(-9)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{13}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}+(-9)\\cdot \\frac{1}{13}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}(4\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}+(-9)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}(4\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 9\\end{bmatrix}+(-9)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}9 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}\\begin{bmatrix}-65 & 78 \\\\ 78 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "55f4143a-4a93-5e8d-b696-93cfb4a2e6c1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511668}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511632 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162646 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1556dc1b-0c8b-54e0-a340-18371f592ed3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407520} {:answer/id #uuid "286d06a8-1050-59ed-a665-558d840c2aae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407525} {:answer/id #uuid "d6effcc8-e643-5b20-9090-b3855faacab9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407541} {:answer/id #uuid "e9f4f75f-447a-5e0c-a1d6-feae985a30a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407543} {:answer/id #uuid "f93a6cf9-78b6-546d-ba88-361fc48a056a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407544}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407544} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebade115-5b0c-52d7-94cc-8ec9e5babad9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407549}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "11bdef24-ff68-513b-bfa6-5f2fb8c78b02" :question/math-academy-id "q-94768" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4$ of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =-4.$ Computing $A-(-4)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+4I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}+4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=\\sqrt{5}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7f7d44ce-dbe3-5404-92b7-3a9a5ceebe0e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511615}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511668 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511495 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a9ba5aa-7ff7-5a13-8435-4ae5f003f1ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510800} {:answer/id #uuid "253fb856-2658-5841-900d-3d095bc94cf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510861} {:answer/id #uuid "4986bc0c-e982-51ac-aecd-3824f87d8c94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510945} {:answer/id #uuid "631ec9ac-1a1a-52f7-bf42-8397061beb8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510992} {:answer/id #uuid "ea35d22f-50f1-5cf4-aa38-4adac074dc4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511270}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511270} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4cd93bf-3a8c-5f18-b352-8eff8b26fc90" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511411}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "524b5d0d-afb1-56e2-9f18-7dc1697e2495" :question/math-academy-id "q-95728" :question/problem "Suppose that\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\n\nare eigenvectors of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ respectively. Find the matrix $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1\\end{aligned}]+(-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1\\end{aligned}]+(-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1\\end{aligned}]+(-2)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}((3)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]+(-2)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}10 & -10 \\\\ -10 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d68ef3a3-b3f5-5888-8b3c-31c4716078a7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511679}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511679 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511494 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a0018a6-7dae-54cf-88b4-02338b8fff5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510914} {:answer/id #uuid "a218fed0-75a7-50d9-8f60-c86aeaebfd23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511121} {:answer/id #uuid "a24957bf-c3bd-5b14-b0b3-55b5967d855a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511123} {:answer/id #uuid "bd5b03f7-2a8e-557c-abde-00dafe7a50cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511173} {:answer/id #uuid "e533b56d-0765-5385-b5e3-a0e0dfcc5a8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511264}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510914} :answer-field/id #uuid "29cd7cba-3362-549f-8e3e-97da0f1c1325" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511333}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4d6df4b5-e301-58fd-845a-847c7a249d39" :question/math-academy-id "q-140849" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =4.$ Computing $A-4I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(\\frac{2}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=\\sqrt{13}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f8df9c63-69f3-5acb-8e84-5b34d208def1"}] :activity/title "The Spectral Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510766}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046645} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544933} :activity/id #uuid "0ee7b968-1996-5dc0-bce6-494a4e054d04" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049849 :topic/id #uuid "1887f465-a1b8-5370-82f6-80e02b9d153a" :topic/math-academy-id 3120} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544908 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365052 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb7dde31-bde1-58e8-ad71-ec6b2e8b2319" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451311} {:answer/id #uuid "3ca9aaad-54c8-5fad-9a03-14113c01dc8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451312}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451311} :answer-field/id #uuid "e741fb79-46a4-593a-9d56-3df9c7384c94" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452503} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05cc96c4-9bd0-5ec2-9c67-70c12fe56748" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451313} {:answer/id #uuid "71be77cb-f2a0-5da9-a71b-0d54bf22920e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451314}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451313} :answer-field/id #uuid "4fda6962-d111-5d52-9d97-94bb5cf1b992" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452504} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "757e1e41-d4bd-5b02-ae6c-09acb0b026a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451315} {:answer/id #uuid "ae88a715-f3a8-55f4-890a-5a6165ebc0af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451316}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451316} :answer-field/id #uuid "c19a4f3d-993f-546f-aa3d-c187f50b1a7a" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452505}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "bb8cc465-a001-5ad5-bfcd-11eb17855f11" :question/math-academy-id "q-140844" :question/problem "Given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ real symmetric matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A$ is not diagonalizable\n- **II.** $A$ must have $4$ integer eigenvalues\n- **III.** If  $\\alpha $ is an eigenvalue of $A$ with the multiplicity $2,$ then the dimension of the eigenspace for the eigenvalue $\\alpha $ is $2.$" :question/worked-solution "Given that $A$ is an $n\\times n$ symmetric matrix over $R$, the spectral theorem states the following:\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are all real.\n\n- Eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues are orthogonal.\n\n- The dimension of the eigenspace for each eigenvalue equals the multiplicity of that eigenvalue as a root of the characteristic polynomial.\n\n- $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the spectral theorem, any symmetric matrix with real entries is orthogonally diagonalizable, so, $A$ is diagonalizable.\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the spectral theorem, the eigenvalues are real but not necessarily integers.\n\n- Statement III is true. According to the theorem, the dimension of the eigenspace for each eigenvalue equals the multiplicity of that eigenvalue as a root of the characteristic polynomial.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "204942ae-e32e-531e-8f95-9aa08d764a99"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544917 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395052 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a65a271-e819-5161-9c5f-b4658866c25c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{25}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 16\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395054} {:answer/id #uuid "3b601ff3-863f-5502-84d6-13f00e2ad45e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 16\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395055} {:answer/id #uuid "84da8f15-dc10-55f3-97d6-434ca1ff643e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{25}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 16\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395056} {:answer/id #uuid "cdb077ea-553a-5aa6-a64a-7a2b84621b7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\begin{bmatrix}80 & 60 \\\\ 60 & 45\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395057} {:answer/id #uuid "fd2d006a-6b5c-519d-a0ac-32bc5de9d2ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 16\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395058}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395054} :answer-field/id #uuid "3211a6bb-133a-5e40-90d8-fc361c03ff47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395053}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "05f92170-0d3f-5d3a-aa67-7dea2e387ec7" :question/math-academy-id "q-95710" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-15 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -8\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =1.$ Computing $A-1I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-15 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -8\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-16 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-16 & 12 & 0 \\\\ 12 & -9 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{3}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-16 & 12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=4,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=5.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 16\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "33991c0d-45f8-5bde-a9e0-865b009ce6cf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544988}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544928 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378058 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b36379c9-48d8-5cb1-80f0-4b648453256c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378060} {:answer/id #uuid "600f54d6-7fa1-5cb7-8b0b-961230b3ef4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378061} {:answer/id #uuid "cfc06545-06d9-5a6d-b04a-bdc1e2c72d73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 9\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378062} {:answer/id #uuid "f04919e1-818b-5e94-8009-e3cff20a8da4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 9\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378063} {:answer/id #uuid "4b6a25f6-1c57-57ce-9ba5-04288080446d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378064}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378061} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6818491-5219-59f7-95e1-4f023133c8d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378059}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "eab64778-c05e-560d-a0b4-afdd1f4a716c" :question/math-academy-id "q-95706" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =-2.$ Computing $A-(-2)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+2I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{bmatrix}+2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 9 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-3{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=\\sqrt{10}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5621b370-c250-590a-850c-a09cc9236f08" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544917}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544933 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544892 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49409b3e-6cf3-58d5-a30f-981d7b832aaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544246} {:answer/id #uuid "5f102541-4e5b-562c-9b94-e08a3d1c19d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544292} {:answer/id #uuid "884bd029-05ca-5b4a-a6a0-982c739c7b5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544381} {:answer/id #uuid "c1d80d43-4b97-54e9-b8b5-55a67da752e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544501} {:answer/id #uuid "f98d9e4b-b211-526a-8436-d0f20159b86b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544632}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544632} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1086491-d596-5df5-9f5e-b792d5105671" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544746}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "eaf43e73-9a1c-5cad-a4f5-266c54b6fab6" :question/math-academy-id "q-95408" :question/problem "Given that $A$ is a real symmetric matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ must have all real eigenvalues\n\nII. If $u$ and $v$ are two eigenvectors, then $u⊥v$\n\nIII. $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable" :question/worked-solution "Given that $A$ is an $n\\times n$ symmetric matrix over $R$, the spectral theorem states the following:\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are all real.\n\n- Eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues are orthogonal.\n\n- The dimension of the eigenspace for each eigenvalue equals the multiplicity of that eigenvalue as a root of the characteristic polynomial.\n\n- $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the eigenvalues of a symmetric matrix are real.\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the theorem, eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues must be orthogonal, but two eigenvectors corresponding to the same eigenvalue may not be orthogonal.\nThis can occur when the geometric multiplicity of the eigenvaule is greater than $1.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, any symmetric matrix with real entries is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "652a9dac-c9d8-5ab6-956b-06621fefc495" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544928}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544988 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366684 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47b8923f-7afa-5e14-b149-d06f52309cb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366686} {:answer/id #uuid "02cd176f-d7d8-56e4-9747-5567e658dda6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366687} {:answer/id #uuid "4a0c2bac-5abf-5014-936e-ed7fb7b084ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366688} {:answer/id #uuid "970025c4-617f-501a-a55b-b16b187ad5bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366689} {:answer/id #uuid "e3545f21-098a-50f3-a494-4ea653f16e81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366690}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366690} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb8b9480-d64b-50e7-a54e-52af09ad2e90" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366685}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "61a2391d-9048-50a5-89de-abedafa32ffa" :question/math-academy-id "q-95720" :question/problem "Suppose that\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nare eigenvectors of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ respectively. Find the matrix $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(4\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+(-1)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(4\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+(-1)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}15 & -10 \\\\ -10 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "eab4f6a7-357a-59d5-b5d9-5cb4b9754a49" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544908}}] :activity/title "The Spectral Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544069}]}
